# V. Risk factors

#5.1 Tariff and trade policy risks (very high)
- The United States has eliminated de Minimis tax exemption of less than $800, and tariff rates have jumped from 0 per cent to 30-145 per cent
- EU will abolish tax exemption of up to 150 euros in July 2026
- Temu was forced to move from direct mail by air to sea + local warehouse model, with logistical cost structures and timeliness affected
- Countries may continue to strengthen their regulation of low-cost cross-border power suppliers

#5.2 Risk of deteriorating competition patterns (high)
- Domestic: Booby-Scat-enhanced low-price strategy, voice-twitching and fast-signers are growing
- Overseas: price and logistics counterattacks by competitors such as Amazon and SHEIN
- Localization transition challenges: the success of the transition from full-to-sub-trust and local vendors has not yet been validated

#5.3 Risk of a continuous decline in profit margins (high)
- Māori rate has fallen from 63 per cent to 57 per cent, ROE from 39 per cent to 25 per cent, with a downward trend
- 100 billion "re-assemble more" input could further stifle profits.
- Business subsidies and logistics infrastructure inputs may become long-term costs rather than one-time inputs

#5.4 Regulatory and compliance risk (middle-high)
- Global multi-country regulatory fines (KOR, USA FTC, Poland, Hungary)
- European Union Digital Services Act (DSA) compliance requirements for the platform
- Product quality and consumer protection complaints may trigger more stringent regulatory action

##5.5 Information transparency risk (medium)
- Non-disclosure of split domestic/Overseas income, non-disclosure of GMV, non-provision of Temu loss data
- Lack of independent verification by investors of the true performance and value of the various operations

